    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came in because you had dark, tarry stools for 2 days, one episode of vomiting blood, and dizziness.\n\nThe team found you had a bleeding ulcer in your duodenum (the first part of your small bowel). You also have an infection called H. pylori in your stomach."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team did a camera test down your throat (OGD) on Day 1. They injected medicine into the ulcer and put a small clip on it to stop the bleeding.\n\nYou were given 2 units of blood.\n\nYou were also given a drip to reduce stomach acid.\n\nA blood-thinning medicine (apixaban) was stopped during your stay.\n\nA test during the camera test confirmed the H. pylori infection."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "Your GP will:\n- Check your blood count again\n- Check that the H. pylori infection has cleared\n- Make a decision about whether to restart your blood-thinning medicine (apixaban)\n\nYou have a gastroenterology clinic appointment and a repeat camera test (OGD) at 6–8 weeks to check that the ulcer has healed."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{SAFETY_NET}}"
        }
      ],
